# Teaching scripts, student-facing steps and saint stories for Kindergarten. script = what the teacher says and does; tryit = what the children hear; saintStory = three short paragraphs.
R = {
'K-1-1': dict(
  saintStory="Thérèse Martin was born in France in 1873, the youngest in a big, loving family. Her mother died when she was four, and she leaned on her father and on God very much.\nShe called God her Father and trusted him like a little child trusts a parent who is holding her hand. When she became a nun she wrote that she did not have to do big things. She could do small things, like smiling at a grumpy person, with great love.\nThérèse reminds us that God is not far away or scary. He is the best Father, and we can bring him everything, even the little things.",
  tryit=["Draw the people who love you.", "Trace a big heart around all of them.", "Say it together: “God loves us.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Sit in a circle. Hold up a photo of a family (yours or a picture-book family) and say, “Who takes care of this family? Who makes sure they have food, and a bed, and a hug?” Let three children answer. Then say, “Today we are going to meet Someone who takes care of everyone in the whole world.”",
    teach=["Say: “We have a mommy and a daddy, or a grandma, or someone at home who takes care of us. But there is Someone even bigger. His name is God. Jesus told us we can call him Father. That is what we say in the Our Father: ‘Our Father, who art in heaven.’”", "Say: “A good father loves his children very much. He is always glad when they come to him. That is what God is like. He is good. He loves every one of us. And here is the best thing: he is always with us, even in the dark, even when we are sad, even when Mommy or Daddy is not in the room.”", "Read Psalm 23:1 slowly: “The Lord is my shepherd; I shall not want.” Say: “A shepherd looks after his sheep. God looks after us the same way.” Hold up the picture of the Good Shepherd carrying a lamb."],
    ask=["Who takes care of you at home? (Let each child name someone.)", "What do you think God is like? (Listen for: kind, big, loves me. If a child says scary, gently say: “God is good and he loves us.”)", "Where is God right now? (Listen for: everywhere, with us. Say: “Yes. He is always with us.”)"],
    practice=["Give each child paper and crayons.", "Say: “Draw the people who love you. Take your time.”", "While they draw, walk around and ask each child to tell you about their picture.", "When most are finished, say: “Now draw a big heart all around your family. God loves every one of them. And he loves you.”", "Hold up the pictures together. Say: “God loves us!” and let everyone say it back."],
    close="Stand and make the Sign of the Cross together, slowly. Say: “Father, thank you for loving us. Amen.” Send the drawings home with a note: “Tonight, tell your family: God loves us.”")),

'K-1-2': dict(
  saintStory="Francis was born in Assisi, Italy, about 800 years ago. His father was a rich cloth merchant, but Francis chose to give away his fine clothes and live simply so he could follow Jesus.\nFrancis loved everything God made. He called the sun his brother and the moon his sister, and he wrote a song of thanks for the water, the wind and the fire. Stories say that he preached to the birds, and that animals were gentle with him.\nFrancis shows us that the world is a gift. When we look at a flower or a bird and say “thank you, God,” we are doing what Saint Francis did.",
  tryit=["Look carefully at what God made.", "Pick one thing and say, “Thank you, God, for ____.”", "Listen to the others’ thank-yous."],
  script=dict(
    open="Before the children arrive, put a few things from outdoors on a tray under a cloth: a leaf, a smooth stone, a feather, a pinecone or shell. Say, “I have some surprises. Each one was made by someone very wonderful.” Lift the cloth and let them touch each thing gently.",
    teach=["Say: “Who made the leaf? A person? No! Nobody in a factory made this. God made it. God made the sky and the sea, the animals and the trees, the sun in the day and the moon at night.”", "Tell the story from Genesis 1 in pieces, with a gesture for each day: light (open hands wide), sky and sea (wave arms), land and plants (grow from a crouch), sun and moon, fish and birds (fly your hands), animals and people. After each piece say with the children: “And God saw that it was good.”", "Say: “Why did God make everything? Because he is good, and he wanted to share. He made it all for us to enjoy and take care of. And when we say thank you, God is very happy.”"],
    ask=["What is your favorite thing God made? (Everyone gets a turn.)", "Was it a good world or a bad world when God made it? (Listen for: good! Say: “Very good.”)", "How can we take care of what God made? (Listen for: water the plants, be kind to animals, not break things.)"],
    practice=["Take the children outside, or to a window, or show a few big photographs of nature.", "Say: “Look with your eyes. Look at something for a long time. Find one thing God made.”", "Go around the circle. Each child says, “Thank you, God, for ____.” The others answer, “Thank you, God.”", "Back inside, pass around the leaf, the stone and the feather again. Say: “These are God’s gifts.”"],
    close="Say the grace together: “Bless us, O Lord, and these thy gifts.” Make the Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find one beautiful thing God made and say thank you.”")),

'K-1-3': dict(
  saint="Carlo Acutis: a boy who loved God and computers and showed that everyone has gifts.",
  saintStory="Carlo Acutis was born in 1991 and grew up in Milan, Italy. He was a cheerful boy who liked soccer, video games and animals, and he loved Jesus in the Eucharist. He went to Mass almost every day.\nCarlo was very good with computers. When he was a teenager, he taught himself to build websites, and he made one that showed Eucharistic miracles from all over the world, so that more people could learn about Jesus in the Eucharist. He was kind to people who were lonely and to those who had less than he did.\nCarlo became very ill when he was fifteen and died in 2006. The Church has named him a saint. He shows us that God makes every child with gifts, and that a child can use their gifts to help others know God.",
  tryit=["Trace your hand.", "Write your name inside it.", "Say: “God made me.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a mirror. Walk around the circle and let each child look at their own face. Say softly, “Look. There is someone God made. There is only one of them in the whole world.”",
    teach=["Say: “When God made the animals, he said, ‘Let the earth bring forth.’ But when he made people, he did something special. He said, ‘Let us make people like us.’ God made you in a very special way, with a body and with a soul that will never die.”", "Say: “You are a person, not a thing. A toy is a thing. A chair is a thing. You are somebody. Nobody else is exactly like you. God knew you before you were born. That is what the psalm says: ‘I am wonderfully made.’”", "Say: “And God made you for a reason: to know him and to love him. He gave you eyes to see his world, ears to hear, hands to help, and a heart to love.”"],
    ask=["What can your hands do? (Listen for: draw, hug, wave, help. Say: “God gave you those hands to love with.”)", "What is something you are good at? (Everyone shares. Make sure every child gets something good said about them.)", "Is anyone else exactly like you? (Listen for: no. Say: “That is why God loves you so much. There is only one you.”)"],
    practice=["Give each child a sheet of paper and a crayon.", "Help each child trace their hand with the other hand holding the crayon.", "Write “God made me” inside the hand (write it lightly for the child to trace over, if you can).", "Let the children decorate the hands with a favorite color.", "Display the hands together on a wall. Say: “Look at all the people God made.”"],
    close="Put your hand on your heart. Say: “Thank you, God, for making me.” Then the Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family one thing God made you good at.”")),

'K-2-1': dict(
  saintStory="Francis of Assisi wanted to help people see what Christmas really means. In December 1223, in the little town of Greccio in Italy, he asked a friend to set up a manger with straw, with an ox and a donkey beside it.\nOn Christmas night the villagers came with torches and candles, and a priest said Mass at the manger. People said they felt as if they were standing at Bethlehem. This is how the first nativity scene came to be, and why we still set one up at Christmas.\nFrancis loved that Jesus, who is God, became a tiny baby for us. When we look at our nativity scene, we can feel what the people of Greccio felt.",
  tryit=["Listen to the story of Jesus’ birth.", "Act it out with the doll and the blanket.", "Sing softly to baby Jesus."],
  script=dict(
    open="Bring a real baby blanket and a doll. Wrap the doll gently and say very quietly, “Shh. Come close. I want to tell you about the most special baby that ever lived.”",
    teach=["Tell the story slowly with pauses: “A long time ago, Mary and Joseph traveled to a town called Bethlehem. There was no room at the inn. So they stayed where the animals sleep. And that night Mary’s baby was born. Her baby was Jesus. She wrapped him in cloths and laid him in a manger, where the animals eat.”", "Say: “Shepherds were out in the fields. Angels came! They sang, ‘Glory to God in the highest!’ The shepherds hurried to Bethlehem and found baby Jesus, just as the angel said.”", "Say: “Here is the greatest secret. This baby is God’s own Son. God came to be a real baby, so that he could be close to us. Jesus is God, and he is also a real person, like us.”"],
    ask=["Where was Jesus born? (Listen for: Bethlehem, where the animals were, a stable.)", "Who came to see Jesus? (Shepherds, angels, Joseph and Mary.)", "Why did God send his Son as a baby? (Listen for: because he loves us, to be with us.)"],
    practice=["Choose children to be Mary, Joseph and the shepherds, and a few to be the angels. The doll is baby Jesus.", "Narrate the story again slowly. Children do the actions: walk to Bethlehem, knock on doors, kneel at the manger.", "Everyone sings softly (“Away in a Manger,” or any gentle carol) while Mary and Joseph hold baby Jesus.", "Rotate roles so that every child gets a part."],
    close="Say the first line of the Hail Mary together: “Hail Mary, full of grace, the Lord is with thee.” Make the Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Put baby Jesus in your nativity scene tonight.”")),

'K-2-2': dict(
  saintStory="Laura Vicuña was born in Chile in 1891. Her father died when she was very small, and her family was very poor. Her mother had to work for a man who was not good to her.\nLaura went to a school run by sisters and learned to love Jesus very much. She prayed every day for her mother. She wanted her mother to come back to the Church and to be happy. Laura offered her life to God for her mother’s sake. She died when she was only twelve.\nShortly before she died, her mother promised to change her life. Laura’s story shows us that a child’s prayers are strong, and that Jesus listens to children.",
  tryit=["Listen to the story of Jesus and the children.", "Use the felt pieces to tell it back.", "Say, “Jesus loves me.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Sit on the floor, not in a chair, and ask the children to sit close. Say, “Today’s story is about a time when some grown-ups said to the children, ‘Go away, Jesus is too busy.’ Do you think he was too busy?”",
    teach=["Tell it: “One day, parents brought their children to Jesus so that he could bless them. The friends of Jesus, called disciples, said, ‘Don’t bother him. He is tired.’ But Jesus was not happy. He said, ‘Let the children come to me. Don’t stop them.’”", "Say: “Then Jesus took the children in his arms. He put his hands on them. And he blessed them. Can you imagine what that was like? Jesus looked at each child with love.”", "Say: “Jesus loves children. That means he loves you, right now. He loves you when you are happy and he loves you when you cry. He loves you when you are good and when you make a mistake. He is never too busy for you.”"],
    ask=["What did the friends of Jesus say? (They said to send the children away.) What did Jesus say? (Let them come!)", "How did Jesus show he loved the children? (He held them and blessed them.)", "What would you say to Jesus if he picked you up? (Take every answer. Say: “You can tell him anything. He always listens.”)"],
    practice=["Set out felt pieces: Jesus, a few children, some grown-ups, a path.", "Retell the story, placing pieces as you tell it. Let children move the pieces.", "Go around: each child holds the Jesus piece, gives it a hug, and says, “Jesus loves me.”", "Everyone sits in a circle. Say: “Jesus loves you. Jesus loves you. Jesus loves every one of you.” Touch each child’s head lightly as you say it, with a blessing."],
    close="Sign of the Cross. Say: “Jesus, thank you for loving us. Amen.” Send home: “Ask someone at home to tell you how much Jesus loves you.”")),

'K-2-3': dict(
  saintStory="Damien de Veuster was born in Belgium in 1840. He became a priest and sailed across the ocean to the islands of Hawaii.\nOn the island of Molokai, people who had a disease called leprosy were sent to live apart, because everyone was afraid of catching it. Father Damien went to live with them. He built homes and a church, dug graves, bandaged sores and said Mass. He did not stay far away. He ate with them and touched them with love.\nAfter some years Damien caught the disease himself, and he died in 1889. He showed that when someone is sick or sad, Jesus wants us to be close, and to help.",
  tryit=["Think of someone who is sick or sad.", "Make a thank-you card for them.", "Pray for them together."],
  script=dict(
    open="Come in with a bandage on one finger, and walk slowly. Say, “Ow. I have a little hurt. Has anyone ever felt sick or sad? What helped you feel better?” Let a few children answer.",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus helped many people. Once, a man was very sick. His skin hurt. Everyone stayed away. But Jesus did not. He touched the sick man, and the man was made well! (Mark 1.) Jesus made sick people well, and he comforted people who were sad.”", "Say: “Once Jesus healed ten men who were sick. Only one came back and said thank you. Jesus was glad he said it. (Luke 17.) When someone helps us, we say thank you.”", "Say: “Jesus is still alive. He still helps us. When we are sick or sad, we can ask him for help. And sometimes he helps us by sending a mom, a dad, a nurse or a friend.”"],
    ask=["How did Jesus help people? (He healed and comforted them.)", "What do we say when someone helps us? (Thank you.)", "Who helps you when you are sick? (Mom, doctor, nurse. Say: “Jesus sends them.”)"],
    practice=["Hand out folded cards, crayons and stickers.", "Say: “Think of someone who is sick, or sad, or lonely. Draw a happy picture for them. We will say thank you to Jesus for helping.”", "Help each child write (or you write) “Jesus loves you” on the inside, and the child’s name.", "Gather the cards in a basket in the middle. Pray together for the people who will receive them."],
    close="Say: “Thank you, Jesus, for helping us. Please help the people who are sick.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Give the card to someone and give them a hug.”")),

'K-3-1': dict(
  saintStory="Juan Diego was an indigenous man who lived near Mexico City about five hundred years ago. One cold morning in December 1531 he was walking to Mass when he heard beautiful singing on a hill called Tepeyac.\nA lady, shining like the sun, spoke to him in his own language. She said she was Mary, the mother of Jesus, and she asked him to tell the bishop to build a church there. The bishop did not believe him at first. Juan Diego carried a bundle of roses in his tilma, a cloak, to show him. When he opened it, the roses fell out, and a beautiful picture of Mary was on the cloth.\nJuan Diego said yes when Mary asked for his help, and that is what Mary did when God asked her. That picture, Our Lady of Guadalupe, is still in Mexico today.",
  tryit=["Listen to how Mary said yes.", "Say the first part of the Hail Mary.", "Draw Mary and baby Jesus."],
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a mother holding her baby. Ask, “Who is holding the baby?” Then show a picture of Mary and Jesus and say, “This mother is very special. She is the mother of Jesus.”",
    teach=["Tell: “One day the angel Gabriel came to a young woman named Mary. He said, ‘Mary, God has chosen you to be the mother of his Son.’ Mary was surprised. She asked a question. And then she said, ‘Yes. Let it be done to me as you say.’ That was her ‘yes.’”", "Say: “Because Mary said yes, Jesus was born. Mary loved baby Jesus, and took care of him. Jesus loved her, and he loves her still.”", "Say: “When Jesus was dying on the cross, he said to his friend John, ‘Here is your mother.’ He gave Mary to all of us. Mary is our mother too. She wants us to love Jesus. She helps us.”"],
    ask=["What did Mary say to God? (Yes.)", "Who is Mary? (The mother of Jesus. And our mother too.)", "What do you say to your mom when she asks you to help? (Listen. Say: “Mary said yes to God. We can say yes too.”)"],
    practice=["Teach the Hail Mary’s first line, in short pieces, children repeating: “Hail Mary,” “full of grace,” “the Lord is with thee.”", "Say it three times, slowly, with hands folded.", "Give each child paper and crayons. Say: “Draw Mary holding baby Jesus.”", "Let children show their pictures and say, “This is Mary, and this is Jesus.”"],
    close="Say the first line of the Hail Mary once more. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say Hail Mary tonight with someone you love.”")),

'K-3-2': dict(
  saintStory="Nicholas lived in the town of Myra, in what is now Turkey, about 1,700 years ago. He grew up to be a bishop, a leader of the Church, and he loved to help the poor.\nA story told about him is that there was a poor man with three daughters who could not afford a gift to help them marry. In the night, Nicholas secretly tossed a bag of gold through the window. The family was able to be happy. Nicholas did not want anyone to know it was him. He gave in secret, so that only God would see.\nOver the years people told this story, and Nicholas became “Santa Claus.” He was one of the saints, a friend of God, who loved to give gifts in secret.",
  tryit=["Look at the pictures of saints.", "Match each saint with a fact.", "Say, “Saint ___, pray for us.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a photo album or a framed family photo and say, “Look at my friends and family. I love them, and I think about them. God has friends, too. We call them saints.”",
    teach=["Say: “A saint is a person who loved God very much. Some saints lived a long time ago, and some lived just a few years ago. Some were kings and some were kids, some were rich and some were poor. But they all loved Jesus most of all.”", "Say: “Where are the saints now? They are in heaven with God. They are very happy. And here is a wonderful secret: the saints pray for us. When we say, ‘Saint Nicholas, pray for us,’ Saint Nicholas talks to God about us. It is like asking a good friend to put in a good word.”", "Say: “You can be a saint too! God made you to be with him in heaven someday. Every day you can try to love God and be kind.”"],
    ask=["What is a saint? (A person who loved God a lot and is in heaven.)", "What do the saints do for us? (They pray for us.)", "Do you know the name of a saint? (Many will say their own names. Say: “Maybe you have a patron saint!”)"],
    practice=["Show pictures of three saints (Nicholas, Francis, Thérèse or ones the children know) and give one simple fact about each.", "Lay out three fact cards face down: “gave gifts secretly,” “loved animals,” “loved God like a little child.”", "Ask a child to turn over a card, read it with your help, and match it to a saint’s picture.", "Say together after each match: “Saint ____, pray for us.”"],
    close="Say the Guardian Angel prayer’s first line, or “Saint ____, pray for us” for each saint. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask your family to tell you about your name saint.”")),

'K-3-3': dict(
  saintStory="Francesco Forgione was born in southern Italy in 1887. As a boy he was quiet and loved to pray. At fifteen he joined the Capuchin friars and took the name Pio.\nPadre Pio was a priest for many years in the little town of San Giovanni Rotondo. Long lines of people came to confession to him and to ask him to pray for them. He spent many hours in the confessional, and he said Mass with deep love. He suffered a lot, and people believed he carried the wounds of Jesus on his hands and feet.\nPeople say Padre Pio often told those who wrote to him that he would send their guardian angel to help. His life reminds us that God gives every one of us an angel.",
  tryit=["Learn the Guardian Angel prayer.", "Do the hand motions.", "Say it quietly with a friend."],
  script=dict(
    open="Have a grown-up helper stand behind the group quietly, out of sight. Say, “I feel like someone is looking after you. Do you feel someone is here?” Reveal the helper with a smile. Say, “God has given each of you someone who is always looking after you, even when you cannot see him.”",
    teach=["Say: “God made angels before he made us. Angels are God’s helpers. They have no bodies like we do. They love God and listen to him. They bring God’s messages. Remember the angel Gabriel who came to Mary?”", "Say: “God has given each of us a special angel, who stays with us always. We call him a guardian angel. He guards us. He does not go away. When we are scared or sad, we can ask him for help.”", "Say: “Jesus said that the children’s angels always see the face of the Father in heaven. So every child is very, very precious.”"],
    ask=["Who are the angels? (God’s helpers.)", "Who does God give to each of us? (A guardian angel.)", "When can we ask our angel for help? (When we are scared, when we are going to bed, when we are far from home.)"],
    practice=["Teach the Guardian Angel prayer a line at a time with motions: Angel of God (arms up like wings), my guardian dear (hand on heart), to whom God’s love commits me here (point up, then to yourself), ever this day (circle arm like the sun), be at my side (hand at side), to light and guard (hold hands like a candle), to rule and guide (hold hands out like a guide). Amen.", "Say it twice with motions, then once while quiet.", "Pair up children and let each pair say it to each other."],
    close="Say the prayer once more. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say the Guardian Angel prayer at bedtime tonight.”")),

'K-4-1': dict(
  saintStory="Agnes Gonxha was born in 1910 in what is now North Macedonia. When she grew up she became a nun and took the name Teresa. She went to India to teach, and then one day she heard Jesus calling her to serve the poorest people.\nIn the city of Calcutta she began to take care of people who were sick, hungry and dying, and who were lying in the streets. She started a family of sisters, the Missionaries of Charity, who cared for them with love. Many people joined her, and houses opened all over the world.\nMother Teresa died in 1997 and was named a saint in 2016. She said the poor are Jesus in disguise. The Church is a family that takes care of everyone, just as she did.",
  tryit=["Look at the people who help at our parish.", "Say each person’s name.", "Thank them in a prayer."],
  script=dict(
    open="Show a large paper family tree or a family photo. Ask, “Who is in this family?” Then say, “We belong to another family, too. It is bigger than any family you know. It is called the Church.”",
    teach=["Say: “On the day you were baptized, God made you part of his family. The Church is God’s family. In this family, God is our Father, and we are all brothers and sisters.”", "Say: “Who helps in our family, the Church? Father, our priest, who says Mass and forgives sins. Deacons and sisters. The people who teach you, sing, take up the collection, clean the church and welcome people at the door. And you!”", "Say: “The first Christians in Jerusalem were a family. They prayed together, shared what they had and broke bread together (Acts 2). We do those things too: we pray together, we share, and we go to Mass.”"],
    ask=["Who is in God’s family? (Everyone who is baptized.)", "Who is the priest at our parish? (Say his name together.)", "How can you help our parish family? (Welcome someone, share, be quiet at Mass, pray for others.)"],
    practice=["Take a short walk around the church or parish building, or look at photos of the people who work there.", "At each stop say the person’s name and what they do: “This is Father ___. He says Mass.”", "Let children give a wave or a thank-you.", "Sit and say together: “Thank you, God, for our family, the Church.”"],
    close="Hold hands in a circle. Say: “We are God’s family.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family one person who helps at church.”")),

'K-4-2': dict(
  saintStory="Tarcisius lived in Rome about 1,700 years ago, in a time when it was dangerous to be a Christian. He was a young boy who served at Mass.\nOne day the priest needed someone to carry the Eucharist to Christians who were in prison, so they could receive Jesus. Tarcisius said he would do it. He held the Eucharist close to his chest and walked through the streets. Some bullies stopped him and asked what he was carrying. He would not show them, and he would not let them take Jesus.\nTarcisius died protecting the Eucharist. He shows us how much to respect Jesus in the church, and why we are quiet and careful there.",
  tryit=["Walk quietly into the church.", "Make the Sign of the Cross and genuflect.", "Sit still and listen."],
  script=dict(
    open="Put a cloth over something very special (a framed photo of the tabernacle, or a small gold box). Say very softly, “I have something very special. We must be very quiet and careful with it.” Lift the cloth slowly. “This is where Jesus lives in our church.”",
    teach=["Say: “A church is not like other buildings. It is God’s house. Jesus is truly there, in the tabernacle. That is why we are quiet, and why we are gentle.”", "Point out, with pictures or in the real church: the altar (where Mass is said), the cross (to remember that Jesus died for us), the tabernacle with its little red light (it shows that Jesus is there), and the holy water (to remember our Baptism).", "Say: “When we come in, we dip our fingers in holy water and make the Sign of the Cross. When we pass the tabernacle, we genuflect, which means we touch one knee to the floor to say hello to Jesus. In the pew we sit, stand and kneel quietly.”"],
    ask=["Whose house is the church? (God’s.)", "How do we act in God’s house? (Quietly, kindly, gently.)", "What does the red light by the tabernacle mean? (Jesus is here.)"],
    practice=["If you can, go into the church. If not, make an “aisle” with tape on the floor and a pretend tabernacle at the end.", "Teach three actions: dip fingers and make the Sign of the Cross, genuflect, and sit with hands folded.", "Let each child walk the aisle slowly, one at a time, while the others watch and say “Welcome, Jesus.”", "Finish by sitting very quietly for twenty seconds."],
    close="Say: “Jesus, we are glad to be in your house.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Next Sunday, show your family how to genuflect.”")),

'K-4-3': dict(
  saintStory="Imelda Lambertini was born in Bologna, Italy, in 1322. She was a girl who loved Jesus very much, and her parents sent her to live with Dominican sisters when she was nine. She loved to pray in front of the tabernacle.\nIn those days children usually received First Communion when they were older, and Imelda longed to receive Jesus. She wished with all her heart that she could. On the day of Ascension, in 1333, when she was eleven, a priest gave her Holy Communion. She was filled with joy. She died soon after, still smiling, right after receiving Jesus.\nImelda is the patron saint of First Communicants. She helps us see how wonderful Sunday Mass is, when we get to be near Jesus.",
  tryit=["Look at the things we see at Mass.", "Say the name of each one.", "Put them in the Sunday basket."],
  script=dict(
    open="Come in with a basket covered by a cloth. Say, “I have a Sunday basket. It has things we see and use at Mass. Can anyone guess what is inside?” Take out one item at a time (a small cross, a candle, a children’s Mass book, a coin for the collection, a missal ribbon) and ask what it is.",
    teach=["Say: “Sunday is a special day. On the first Easter Sunday, Jesus rose from the dead. That is why Christians gather every Sunday, to say thank you and to celebrate Jesus. The psalm says: ‘This is the day the Lord has made. Let us rejoice and be glad.’”", "Say: “At Mass we do many things. We sing. We listen to God’s word. We pray. We bring gifts. And then Jesus comes to us in Holy Communion. We say thank you to God.”", "Say: “Going to Mass is not like going to a show. It is like coming to a family dinner. Jesus invites us, and he is the host.”"],
    ask=["What day do we go to Mass? (Sunday.)", "What do we do at Mass? (Sing, pray, listen, thank God.)", "Why do we call Sunday a special day? (Jesus rose on a Sunday.)"],
    practice=["Pass around each item in the basket. Say its name and what it is for.", "Let the children put each item in a shoebox “Sunday basket” while you say, “At Mass we use a ___.”", "Teach one simple response, such as “Thanks be to God.” Practice it three times.", "Close the basket and say: “On Sunday, God is waiting for us.”"],
    close="Say: “This is the day the Lord has made.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “On Sunday, look for one thing from our basket at Mass.”")),

'K-5-1': dict(
  saintStory="Lucy lived on the island of Sicily about 1,700 years ago, when it was dangerous to be a Christian. Her name means “light.”\nShe belonged to a rich family, but she gave away her goods to help the poor. She would not give up her faith in Jesus, even when she was told that she must. She stayed faithful until she died. People tell that she brought food to Christians who were hiding underground and wore a crown of candles on her head to light the way, so her hands were free to carry food.\nLucy’s feast day, December 13, is celebrated with lights. At Baptism we receive a candle that is lit from the Easter candle. It says that Christ is our light, as he was for Lucy.",
  tryit=["Look at a baptism candle.", "Light the LED candle and say: “Jesus is my light.”", "Blow it out, then light it again."],
  script=dict(
    open="Dim the lights and put a baptism candle (or an LED candle) in the middle of the circle. Say, “Everyone, watch the candle. What does the light do?” Turn it on. “It makes the dark go away. A very special candle was lit for you on the day of your Baptism.”",
    teach=["Say: “When you were a baby, your family took you to church. The priest poured water on your head, and said, ‘I baptize you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.’ And you became a child of God. God’s own life came into you. That is called grace.”", "Say: “You were wrapped in a white garment, to show that you were clean and new. And a candle was lit from the big Easter candle. The priest said, ‘Receive the light of Christ.’ That candle means: Jesus is our light, and we should shine too.”", "Say: “Jesus told his friends to baptize everyone: ‘Go and baptize them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.’ That is what we say when we make the Sign of the Cross. It reminds us of Baptism.”"],
    ask=["What happens at Baptism? (Water, words, God’s family, God’s life.)", "What do you see in the candle? (Light. Jesus is our light.)", "What words are said at Baptism? (In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.)"],
    practice=["Show a real baptism candle if possible, or the photo of the child’s own baptism.", "Give each child an LED candle. Say: “This is not a real flame, so it is safe.”", "Say, as you turn on the light: “Jesus is my light.” Then switch it off and say, “I can shine for Jesus.”", "Turn it on again together.", "Let children shine their candles for each other and say, “You are a child of God.”"],
    close="Hold the candles up and say: “Thank you, God, for making us your children.” Make the Sign of the Cross slowly. Send home: “Ask to see your baptism photo, candle or gown.”")),

'K-5-2': dict(
  saint="Pius X: the pope who invited children to receive Jesus in Holy Communion.",
  saintStory="Giuseppe Sarto was born in 1835 in the small Italian village of Riese, into a poor family. As a boy he walked several miles to school each day, and he carried his shoes in his hand to save them for church. He became a priest, then a bishop, and in 1903 he became pope and took the name Pius X.\nPope Pius X loved the Eucharist very much, and he wanted everyone to receive Jesus often. In 1910 he said that children could make their First Communion when they were about seven years old, as soon as they could understand that the bread is Jesus. Before that, many children had to wait until they were much older.\nBecause of Pius X, children all over the world can receive Jesus early. His life shows that Jesus is a gift for the young, and that we should get our hearts ready.",
  tryit=["Show reverent hands.", "Practice walking and kneeling reverently.", "Draw an altar with a chalice."],
  script=dict(
    open="Show a loaf of bread and a cup of grape juice. Say, “What are these? Bread and juice. We eat them for lunch. But at Mass, something amazing happens to bread and wine. Let me tell you.”",
    teach=["Say: “The night before Jesus died, he ate a meal with his friends, called the Last Supper. He took bread, blessed it, broke it, and said, ‘Take this, all of you, and eat it. This is my body.’ Then he took the cup and said, ‘This is my blood.’ And he said, ‘Do this in memory of me.’” (Luke 22:19)", "Say: “The priest does what Jesus did, at every Mass. He says Jesus’ own words, and the bread and wine become Jesus. They still look the same, but they are really Jesus. That is a big mystery. We call it the Eucharist.”", "Say: “Jesus gives himself to us. When the children are older, they can receive Jesus in Holy Communion. Now we are getting our hearts ready. How? We learn about Jesus, we pray, we say sorry when we do something wrong, and we are kind.”"],
    ask=["What happens to the bread and wine at Mass? (They become Jesus.)", "Why does Jesus give himself to us? (Because he loves us.)", "How can we get ready to receive Jesus? (Pray, be kind, say sorry.)"],
    practice=["Show how to hold hands as a throne (left over right, palms up) and fold hands to show respect.", "Practice walking slowly, hands folded, to the front of the room and back.", "Give paper and crayons. Say: “Draw an altar with a cup, a chalice.”", "As children draw, say the prayer softly: “Jesus, I love you. Come into my heart.”"],
    close="Say the prayer together: “Jesus, I love you. Come into my heart.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask when you can see the tabernacle at church.”")),

'K-5-3': dict(
  saintStory="Maria Goretti was born in Italy in 1890 to a poor farming family. When her father died, her mother worked hard to feed the children, and Maria helped by cooking, cleaning and caring for her little brothers and sisters.\nShe was a gentle, prayerful girl, and she was looking forward to making her First Communion. One day, when she was eleven, a young man named Alessandro, who lived near her, did a terrible thing to her. She was hurt so badly that she died the next day. Before she died, Maria forgave him and said she wanted him in heaven with her.\nAlessandro was in prison for many years and was very sorry. He asked for forgiveness from Maria’s mother, who forgave him. Maria shows how strong forgiveness is, and that God always forgives when we are sorry.",
  tryit=["Listen to the story of the lost son.", "Practice: “I’m sorry. I forgive you.”", "Hug your partner."],
  script=dict(
    open="Read a short picture book where a character says sorry. Or say, “Do you know what to do when you make a mistake? I spilled juice on a book today, and I felt so bad.” Tell the story and ask, “What should I do?”",
    teach=["Tell the story from Luke 15 in your own words: “A man had two sons. The younger one said, ‘Father, give me my share.’ He went far away and spent all his money. Then he had nothing to eat. He said, ‘I will go back and say, I am sorry.’ And while he was still far away, his father saw him, ran to him, hugged him and kissed him. The father said, ‘Let’s have a party! My son was lost, and now he’s found.’”", "Say: “The father in the story is like God. When we do something wrong, God is not angry forever. When we say sorry, he runs to us. He hugs us and forgives us. God’s love is bigger than our mistakes.”", "Say: “When we do something wrong, we feel sad inside. We can tell God, ‘I am sorry.’ And God forgives. Later we will learn about a sacrament called Reconciliation, where a priest helps us.”"],
    ask=["What did the son do wrong? (He took the money and spent it, and left.)", "What did the father do when the son came back? (He ran, hugged and forgave him.)", "What can we say to God when we do something wrong? (I’m sorry.)"],
    practice=["Choose a simple pretend situation: “Someone knocks down your tower.”", "Show two children how to say “I’m sorry” and “I forgive you,” with a hug or handshake.", "Let each pair practice. Walk around and help.", "Come back together and say as a group: “Jesus, I am sorry. Please forgive me.”"],
    close="Sign of the Cross. Say: “Thank you, God, for forgiving us.” Send home: “Say sorry to someone this week, and forgive someone too.”")),

'K-6-1': dict(
  saintStory="Martin de Porres was born in Lima, Peru, in 1579. His father was a Spanish nobleman and his mother was a freed woman from Panama, and some people were unkind to him because of the color of his skin.\nMartin became a Dominican brother, and the sisters and brothers soon discovered how much he cared for people. He worked in the infirmary, caring for the sick, whether they were rich or poor, and he gave his food to those who were hungry. He also loved animals, and it is said that a dog, a cat and a mouse ate from the same dish at his feet.\nMartin shows us how to love God and love others: be kind, share, and help, no matter who people are.",
  tryit=["Think of a kind thing you can do.", "Write or draw it on a paper link.", "Add your link to the chain."],
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a heart cut from paper. Say, “Jesus told us the two most important things in the whole world. They both have the word ‘love’ in them. Can you guess what they are?” Let children guess, then say: “Love God, and love other people.”",
    teach=["Say: “One day someone asked Jesus, ‘What is the most important thing God wants us to do?’ Jesus said, ‘Love the Lord your God with all your heart. And love your neighbor as yourself.’ (Mark 12:29-31)”", "Say: “We love God when we pray, say thank you, go to Mass and listen to him. We love others when we share, take turns, help, include a lonely child, and use kind words. Whenever we do something kind for someone, we are loving Jesus, too.”", "Say: “Love is not just a feeling. Love is something we do. A kind act is a way of saying, ‘I love you.’”"],
    ask=["What are the two most important things? (Love God. Love others.)", "How can we show love to someone at school? (Share, play together, help.)", "What is one kind thing you did this week? (Let several share.)"],
    practice=["Cut strips of colored paper.", "Say: “Think of one kind thing you can do this week.” Help each child tell you or draw it on a strip.", "Make a loop with each strip and link them into a chain, one link at a time.", "Hang the chain on the wall. Add a link each time a child does something kind."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, help us love you and love each other.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do one kind thing every day and tell us about it.”")),

'K-6-2': dict(
  saintStory="Thomas More was an English lawyer and writer who lived about 500 years ago. He was known to be honest and wise, and the king, Henry VIII, made him an important leader of the country. He was also a loving father to his children.\nWhen the king wanted Thomas to say something that he did not believe was true, Thomas refused. He said what he believed, even though it would cost him. The king put him in prison, and later Thomas was executed. His last words were that he was “the king’s good servant, but God’s first.”\nThomas More was named a saint. He shows us that the truth matters more than being safe, and that God helps those who tell the truth.",
  tryit=["Listen to the story of honest Tom.", "Act it out with a puppet.", "Say: “The truth will set you free.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Use two hand puppets. One puppet says, “I did not break the cup!” with a very sneaky voice while holding a broken cup behind it. Ask the children: “Is the puppet telling the truth? How do you know?”",
    teach=["Say: “God wants us to tell the truth. One of God’s ten rules says, ‘You shall not tell lies.’ (Exodus 20:16) When we tell a lie, we say something that is not true, and that hurts people, because they cannot trust us.”", "Say: “Sometimes we are scared to tell the truth, because we think we will be in trouble. But Jesus said, ‘The truth will set you free.’ (John 8:32) When we tell the truth, we feel light and free, like we put down something heavy.”", "Say: “Telling the truth is brave. It is okay to say, ‘I did it, and I am sorry.’ God is proud of us when we tell the truth, and the people who love us are, too.”"],
    ask=["What is a lie? (Saying something that is not true.)", "Why is it hard to tell the truth sometimes? (We are scared of trouble.)", "How do you feel after telling the truth? (Better, free, happy.)"],
    practice=["Read a short picture book in which a character tells the truth when it is hard (for example, about a broken vase or a missing cookie).", "Give the children the two puppets. Let pairs act out: puppet 1 breaks something, puppet 2 asks what happened.", "Practice the brave answer: “I did it. I’m sorry.”", "Cheer for each pair."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, help us tell the truth.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell the truth this week, even if it’s hard.”")),

'K-6-3': dict(
  saintStory="Maria Goretti was an Italian girl who lived about 120 years ago. Her family was poor, and Maria worked hard at home and prayed a lot.\nWhen she was eleven, a young man named Alessandro hurt her very badly. In the hospital she was in great pain, but she said that she forgave him and that she wanted him to be with her in heaven. She died the next day.\nYears later Alessandro was sorry. Maria’s forgiveness helped him change his heart, and he asked God and her family for forgiveness. Maria teaches us that forgiving is very strong, and God helps us do it.",
  tryit=["Say: “I’m sorry.”", "Answer: “I forgive you.”", "Shake hands or give a hug."],
  script=dict(
    open="Have two dolls or puppets: one takes the other’s toy. Say, “Oh no. Now what should happen? The first puppet is sorry. The second one is sad. What should they say?”",
    teach=["Say: “Sometimes people hurt us, and sometimes we hurt people. Both are very hard. But Jesus shows us what to do. When we hurt someone, we say sorry. When someone hurts us, we forgive.”", "Tell: “Peter once asked Jesus, ‘How many times should I forgive someone? Seven times?’ And Jesus said, ‘Not seven times, but seventy times seven.’ That means always, over and over.” (Matthew 18:21-22)", "Say: “We pray this in the Our Father: ‘Forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us.’ Jesus forgives us, so we can forgive, too. It is not easy. But Jesus helps us.”"],
    ask=["What do we say when we hurt someone? (I’m sorry.)", "What do we say when someone says sorry? (I forgive you.)", "Who helps us forgive? (Jesus.)"],
    practice=["Teach the two phrases: “I’m sorry.” and “I forgive you.” Say them with actions (hand on heart, then open hands).", "Pair up children. Say: “One of you pretends to bump the other. The other says, ‘That’s okay, I forgive you.’”", "Let partners switch roles.", "Come back and give each child a sticker of a heart, saying, “Forgiving is brave.”"],
    close="Say the line from the Our Father: “Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “If someone says sorry to you, say, ‘I forgive you.’”")),

'K-7-1': dict(
  saintStory="Bernadette Soubirous was born in 1844 in the town of Lourdes, France. Her family was very poor, and Bernadette was often sick. She could not read well, but she knew how to pray the Rosary.\nIn 1858, when she was fourteen, she was gathering firewood near a cave when a beautiful lady appeared to her. The lady held a rosary and smiled. Over the next months, the lady came back eighteen times and asked Bernadette to pray for sinners. Bernadette prayed and obeyed what she was asked. A spring of water began to flow from the cave floor, and many sick people have been healed.\nBernadette became a sister, and she is a saint. She teaches us that anyone can pray, even someone small and poor, and that God hears.",
  tryit=["Be very still for one minute.", "Listen in your heart.", "Say: “Thank you, God. I love you.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Start with a surprise: whisper, and ask the children to whisper back. “God can hear whispers. Today we’re going to talk about how to talk to God.”",
    teach=["Say: “Prayer is talking to God, and listening to him, too. God is always there. He never says, ‘Not now.’ He always listens. We can pray at any time, in any place.”", "Say: “There are many ways to pray. We can say words we have learned, like the Sign of the Cross. We can sing. We can talk to God in our own words: ‘Thank you, God. I am sorry. Please help my friend.’ And we can be quiet, and just be with God, like sitting next to someone you love.”", "Say: “Jesus said that when we pray, we should go to a quiet place, and the Father who sees us will hear. He loves it when we talk to him.” (Matthew 6:6)"],
    ask=["What is prayer? (Talking to God.)", "When can we pray? (Anytime, anywhere.)", "What would you like to say to God today? (Let several answer.)"],
    practice=["Sit comfortably with eyes closed, or looking at a candle or a crucifix.", "Say: “We will be quiet for one minute and think about God.” Keep time.", "After one minute, say: “Now say it softly after me: ‘Thank you, God.’ ‘I love you.’”", "Let children who want to add their own words do so."],
    close="Sign of the Cross. Say: “God always listens.” Send home: “Pray with your family at bedtime tonight.”")),

'K-7-2': dict(
  saintStory="Teresa was born in Avila, Spain, in 1515. When she was a girl she loved to read adventure stories, and she once ran away from home with her brother to find the land of the martyrs. They did not get far. She became a Carmelite nun and spent many years finding it hard to pray.\nLater Teresa learned to pray from the heart, and she started new, simple convents where sisters prayed and lived by their faith. She wrote books about prayer, including a book in which she thinks about each line of the Our Father. She is called a Doctor of the Church because she taught so many people how to pray.\nTeresa tells us that when we say the Our Father slowly, and think about the words, we are really talking with God.",
  tryit=["Say the Our Father one line at a time.", "Do the hand motions with the class.", "Say it again, very slowly."],
  script=dict(
    open="Ask the children, “If you wanted to ask your friend to teach you to ride a bike, what would you say?” Then, “The friends of Jesus asked him, ‘Lord, teach us to pray.’ And Jesus gave them a prayer.”",
    teach=["Say: “This prayer is called the Our Father. Jesus himself gave us these words, so it is a very special prayer. We pray it at Mass, at home and at school. It begins: ‘Our Father, who art in heaven.’ That means ‘God, you are our Father, and you are in heaven.’”", "Go slowly through the parts in simple words. “‘Hallowed be thy name’ means: we say your name with love. ‘Thy kingdom come’ means: may everyone love you. ‘Give us this day our daily bread’ means: please give us what we need. ‘Forgive us our trespasses’ means: forgive us when we do wrong. ‘Deliver us from evil’ means: keep us safe.”", "Say: “It is a gift from Jesus. When we pray it, we say Jesus’ own words to God, our Father.”"],
    ask=["Who gave us the Our Father? (Jesus.)", "Who is the ‘Father’ in this prayer? (God.)", "What do we ask God for? (Bread, forgiveness, to be safe.)"],
    practice=["Teach one line at a time, with hand motions: “Our Father” (hands up), “who art in heaven” (point up), “hallowed be thy name” (hands folded), “thy kingdom come” (arms wide), “thy will be done” (hand on heart), “give us this day our daily bread” (hands out, cupped), “forgive us our trespasses” (hands on chest), “as we forgive those who trespass against us” (hug yourself), “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil” (hold hands out like a shield). Amen.", "Repeat each line three times, children copying.", "Say the whole prayer slowly with all the motions.", "Let a few children lead the group."],
    close="Say the whole prayer one more time, slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say the Our Father with your family at dinner.”")),

'K-7-3': dict(
  saintStory="Nicholas Herman was born in France in about 1614. He fought as a soldier when he was young, and then he joined the Carmelites in Paris as a brother, taking the name Lawrence.\nBrother Lawrence worked in the monastery kitchen for many years, cooking for the whole community and washing a big pile of dishes. He said that he did not mind. He kept God company while he scrubbed pots and pans, and he called this “practicing the presence of God.” People came to ask him for advice about prayer.\nBrother Lawrence teaches us that we can pray anywhere. When we bless our food and begin the day with the Sign of the Cross, even a plain meal turns into a way to talk to God.",
  tryit=["Make the Sign of the Cross slowly.", "Act out a family meal blessing.", "Say: “Bless us, O Lord.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Set up a pretend table with plates and cups. Ask, “What do we do before we eat?” Let children say. “Today we’ll learn two things we do every day: the Sign of the Cross, and a prayer before meals.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Sign of the Cross is how a Christian begins to pray. We touch our forehead and say, ‘In the name of the Father.’ We touch our chest and say, ‘and of the Son.’ We touch our left shoulder, then our right, and say, ‘and of the Holy Spirit. Amen.’ We are saying: ‘God is Father, Son and Holy Spirit. I belong to him.’”", "Say: “The cross is the sign of how much Jesus loves us. He died on a cross for us. When we make the sign of the cross, we remember this.”", "Say: “Before we eat, we say thank you to God for our food. We call it grace, because God’s gifts are called graces. We say: ‘Bless us, O Lord, and these thy gifts, which we are about to receive, from thy bounty, through Christ our Lord. Amen.’”"],
    ask=["What do we say when we make the Sign of the Cross? (In the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.)", "Why do we say grace? (To thank God for food.)", "Who gives us our food? (God, through people who work.)"],
    practice=["Slowly practice the Sign of the Cross, a part at a time. Use the right hand, touching forehead, chest, left shoulder, right shoulder.", "Stand and do it together three times, once very slowly.", "Seat children at the pretend table. Say: “We are a family. Let’s say grace.”", "Say the grace together, with the Sign of the Cross, then pretend to eat."],
    close="Say the grace together once more. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say grace at dinner tonight.”")),

'K-8-1': dict(
  saintStory="John the Baptist was a cousin of Jesus, born to Elizabeth and Zechariah when they were old. An angel had said that John would get people ready for the Lord.\nWhen John grew up, he lived in the desert, ate locusts and honey, and wore a rough camel-hair cloak. People came from all over to hear him. He told them to be sorry for their sins, share with those in need, and baptize in the Jordan River. He said that someone greater was coming: “I am not worthy to untie his sandals.”\nOne day Jesus came to John, and John said, “Behold, the Lamb of God.” We hear about John every Advent, because he teaches us how to get ready for Jesus.",
  tryit=["Make a paper wreath with four candles.", "Color one flame for this week.", "Say: “Come, Lord Jesus.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Bring a calendar with a day circled, or a paper chain with days crossed off. Ask, “Have you ever waited for something exciting, like a birthday? How did you get ready?”",
    teach=["Say: “Advent is the time before Christmas, when we wait for Jesus. It is four weeks long. We use that time to get our hearts ready. Do you clean your room for guests? We make our hearts ready for Jesus.”", "Show the wreath: “This is an Advent wreath. It is a circle, which has no end, like God’s love. It has four candles: three purple and one pink. The green branches remind us of life. Each week we light one more candle. The light grows and grows until Christmas.”", "Say: “While we wait we pray, ‘Come, Lord Jesus.’ We do kind things. We get ready, like John the Baptist, who said, ‘Get ready, Jesus is coming.’ And we wait with joy.”"],
    ask=["What are we waiting for in Advent? (Jesus’ birthday, Christmas.)", "How many candles are on the wreath? (Four.)", "How can we get ready for Jesus? (Pray, be kind, share.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a paper plate (or cut-out ring), green paper, and four candle shapes (three purple, one pink).", "Help each child glue the candles on a green wreath.", "Color a flame yellow for the first week.", "Add one more flame each week, so the wreath grows."],
    close="Say: “Come, Lord Jesus.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Light the Advent wreath with your family.”")),

'K-8-2': dict(
  saint="Mary Magdalene: the first person to see Jesus alive on Easter morning.",
  saintStory="Mary of Magdala was one of the women who followed Jesus and helped to take care of him and his friends. Jesus had set her free from great trouble, and she loved him very much.\nShe stayed near the cross when most of the friends of Jesus ran away. On the morning of the third day she went to the tomb very early, while it was still dark. The stone had been rolled away, and the tomb was empty. She stood weeping, and then a man spoke her name, “Mary.” She turned and saw it was Jesus! She ran to tell the disciples, “I have seen the Lord!” That is why she is sometimes called the “apostle to the apostles.”\nMary Magdalene shows us what Easter joy is like. The sad time of Lent turned into the greatest news ever told: Jesus is alive.",
  tryit=["Put a coin in the Lenten bank each time you share.", "Decorate an egg with a cross.", "Say: “Alleluia! Jesus is risen!”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Start by hiding a toy under a cloth. “I have something to show you, but first we have to wait.” After a minute, lift the cloth. “Waiting makes it even better. The Church does that with Lent and Easter.”",
    teach=["Say: “Lent is the time before Easter. It is forty days. We pray more, we give to those who need help, and we try to say no to things that are not good for us. It is a time to get ready to celebrate that Jesus rose from the dead.”", "Tell: “On the first Easter morning, some women went to Jesus’ tomb. The stone was rolled away. The tomb was empty! An angel said, ‘He is not here. He has risen!’ (Luke 24) Jesus was alive!”", "Say: “That is why Easter is the happiest day in the whole year. We say ‘Alleluia!’ again, the church is full of flowers, and we celebrate. Lent is a quiet time. Easter is a joyful time.”"],
    ask=["What do we do during Lent? (Pray, share, say no to things.)", "What happened on Easter morning? (Jesus rose from the dead.)", "What is a word we say at Easter? (Alleluia!)"],
    practice=["Give each child a small cup or a paper bank. Say: “This is our Lenten bank. When we do something kind or give up a treat, we put in a coin to help the poor.”", "Decorate a paper egg with a cross and bright colors.", "Hold the eggs up and shout, “Alleluia! Jesus is risen!” Cheer.", "Put the eggs on the wall as a bright display."],
    close="Sign of the Cross. Say: “Jesus, thank you for rising for us.” Send home: “Start saving coins for the poor.”")),

'K-8-3': dict(
  saintStory="Thérèse of Lisieux was a young French girl who became a Carmelite nun when she was fifteen. She was not famous or powerful. She felt small, like a little flower in God’s garden.\nShe wrote that she could not do great things, but she could do small things with great love: smile at a grumpy sister, say thank you, or do a chore quietly. She called this her “little way.” She was very sick for a long time, but kept trusting God. She died when she was twenty-four.\nThérèse is a saint, and a Doctor of the Church. She shows us that in Ordinary Time, when nothing special happens, we can grow in love like a little plant.",
  tryit=["Plant a seed in a cup.", "Water it a little.", "Say: “Help me grow, Lord.”"],
  script=dict(
    open="Bring a small plant in a pot and a seed packet. Ask the children, “How does a seed grow?” Take answers (water, sun, soil, time).",
    teach=["Say: “After Christmas and Easter, the Church has a long season called Ordinary Time. The color is green, like grass and leaves. Green is the color of growing. In Ordinary Time we grow, like plants, a little at a time, in God’s love.”", "Read Psalm 1:3: “Like a tree planted by streams of water.” Say: “A tree grows tall when it has good water. We grow when we pray, go to Mass, are kind and listen to Jesus.”", "Say: “Growing takes time. It does not happen in one day. Every day we can do small things: say a prayer, help someone, say thank you. And God helps us grow, as a gardener cares for the garden.”"],
    ask=["What color is Ordinary Time? (Green.)", "What does a plant need to grow? (Water, sun, soil.)", "What do we need to grow close to God? (Prayer, Mass, kindness.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a clear cup, a spoonful of soil and a bean or other seed.", "Help children plant their seeds and water them gently.", "Write their names on the cups.", "Put the cups in a sunny spot and check them each week."],
    close="Say: “Help me grow, Lord.” Say the grace after meals: “We give thee thanks, almighty God.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look at your seed and say thank you each day.”")),
}
